WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.686
A burrito order on UberEats has three sides.

00:00:05.686 --> 00:00:09.372
The diner, the driver, and the restaurant.

00:00:09.372 --> 00:00:13.058
And the moment the order is placed, all three have to agree.

00:00:13.058 --> 00:00:16.745
UberEats keeps them in one relational database, the shared source of truth.

00:00:16.745 --> 00:00:20.431
Placing an order is a single transaction.

00:00:20.431 --> 00:00:24.117
Charge the diner, assign exactly one driver, decrement inventory.

00:00:24.117 --> 00:00:27.803
All of it, or none of it.

00:00:27.803 --> 00:00:31.489
That atomic guarantee is why two drivers can never claim the same order.

00:00:31.489 --> 00:00:36.218
Look closer at that one order.

00:00:36.218 --> 00:00:40.947
Three records change at once.

00:00:40.947 --> 00:00:45.677
The diner's balance, the driver's assignment, the restaurant's inventory.

00:00:45.677 --> 00:00:50.406
If any one of them fails, none of them should happen.

00:00:50.406 --> 00:00:55.135
That is atomicity, the A in ACID, and it is exactly what a relational database gives you.

00:00:55.135 --> 00:00:59.864
No bespoke coordination code, just one transaction, and the database holds that guarantee even if the server crashes in the middle.

00:00:59.864 --> 00:01:04.105
The apps on each phone hold volatile state.

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:08.347
What you see on screen right now, gone when you close the app.

00:01:08.347 --> 00:01:12.588
The database holds the global state, the truth everyone shares.

00:01:12.588 --> 00:01:16.830
The split matters.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:21.072
The apps can crash, lose signal, or restart, and the order is still correct, because the truth never lived in the app.

00:01:21.072 --> 00:01:25.313
It lived in the database, the one place all three sides agree on.

00:01:25.313 --> 00:01:30.545
There is one more place the database lives: the driver's phone itself.

00:01:30.545 --> 00:01:35.777
The driver app runs an embedded SQLite database, so it keeps working with no signal, transactional even if the phone dies mid-write.

00:01:35.777 --> 00:01:41.010
SQLite is the most widely deployed database in the world for exactly this reason.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:46.242
On reconnect, it pushes its local changes up and reconciles against the shared truth.

00:01:46.242 --> 00:01:51.474
That truth is one cloud database.

00:01:51.474 --> 00:01:56.706
Same SQL, from the data center to your pocket.

00:01:56.706 --> 00:02:02.825
One service, one database, three sides, and a copy in every pocket.

00:02:02.825 --> 00:02:08.943
The relational database is the shared truth that keeps the diner, driver, and restaurant in agreement, and its transactions make that agreement safe.

00:02:08.943 --> 00:02:15.061
From the cluster to the phone, it is the same SQL, and the same transactional guarantees.

00:02:15.061 --> 00:02:21.180
That is the spine this whole course builds on.
